add('V1 SFT task accuracy: consistent80/80, incident37/60, conflict174/200, abstain50/60. All Hindi and Telugu v1 prompts were romanized. All25 Hindi OSS-style failures and7 Telugu failures returned correct scalars and both citations but used `answered` instead of `conflict_resolved` and omitted the stale page: this is a status-contract failure, not evidence of zero Hindi comprehension. Incident failures involved wrong table/key lookup and wrong arithmetic. Unplugged recall18/28 contrasted with missing-entity32/32.\n\nThe v1 cloud SFT trained34 updates, microbatch8×accumulation2,360 training rows/40 validation rows,1.5 requested epochs (544 rounded exposures), assistant-only labels, LoRA r16/alpha32/dropout.05,1536-token budget. The subsequent real60-update G4 GRPO pilot (20+40) had SFT40/40 and GRPO40/40 on only10 validation cases with no abstention items;37/60 groups were all-correct,2all-wrong,21mixed. A ceiling on this limited routing-heavy set did not show GRPO adds general reasoning. No complete matched v1 GRPO holdout gain is claimed. Artifacts: `reports/cloud_grpo/GRPO_EXPERIMENT.md`, `reports/sft_fire_nf4/FINDINGS.md`.')
add('### Experiment 2 — closed-loop v2, weak SFT and abstention collapse\n\nThe pinned BF16 base reached0.50% synthetic/0.00% OSS. One epoch of240 rows at effectivebatch16 produced only15 optimizer steps:15.00%/16.00% holdout,22.50% validation and75%/70% holdout format validity. GRPO used evidence_gated rewards, G4,120Stage1+220Stage2 updates, LR5e−6, beta.04, temperatures.8/.95, exact accepted-kind balance. Stage1 raised validation22.50→43.75% (+21.25pp [6.25,38.75]) and synthetic15.00→26.25% (+11.25pp [5.00,18.00]). This is a measured improvement for the under-exposed policy under this recipe, not proof of compute-matched RL superiority. The preserved Stage1 adapter was subsequently evaluated on both holdouts:26.25% synthetic and26.00% OSS.\n\nAt340 cumulative updates, final accuracy collapsed to17.50%/15.00%; strict stale catch was0% in both. It predicted abstain on309/400 synthetic and85/100 OSS questions, although only60 and15 required abstention. No conflict or incident was correct. Synthetic citation F1 fell38.27→7.06% and chain compliance78.53→7.65%; OSS chain69.41→1.18%. Format validity improved to98.50%/98.00%, demonstrating that formatting alone is a poor agent-success measure. Stage1 accepted120/153 groups (8all-correct and25all-wrong skipped); Stage2 accepted220/655 (182all-correct and253all-wrong skipped). Negative-group inversion can reward refusal relative to risky malformed/stale attempts, then uniformly wrong refusals get skipped for zero advantage. The trace pattern is consistent with this mechanism, but reward, weak warm start and over-optimization were not isolated causally. Old evidence remains unchanged under `reports_v2/`.')
add('### Experiment 3 — four-epoch cloning control and sampled-diversity bottleneck\n\nFresh SFT from base trained120 updates,4epochs, batch8,LR1.5e−4,cosine decay to0,six-step warmup,960exposures,seq2560,0truncations. Validation by epoch:52.50%,67.50%,65.00%,73.75%; predicted abstention15.00%,23.75%,22.50%,22.50%. Best120 reached77.25% synthetic and76.00% OSS. Its G4 anti-collapse pilot retained6/72groups (8.33%);66zero-variance rejects included62identical assistant-token groups,3groups with2unique sequences and1with3. No all-negative rejection was recorded in that persisted72-group pilot. The pilot was discarded and its logs preserved. This supports a sampling/advantage bottleneck, not a measurement of full-distribution entropy or a causal claim that four epochs always cause collapse. Full SFT is a strong behavioral-cloning ablation, not a theoretical performance ceiling.')
add('### Experiment 4 — 60-step warmup and G6 positive-anchor GRPO\n\nFresh warmup trained60updates/2epochs atbatch8 with480exposures, LR1.5e−4, LoRA r16/alpha32/dropout.05,seq2560. It used the first60steps of the120-step cosine horizon rather than forcing decay tozero at60. Both45 and60 satisfied ≥90% protocol validity and preferred55–68% validation accuracy;60 won on validation alone (58.75→67.50%, both100%format), before inspecting holdouts. GRPO trained live full episodes from that adapter, with a frozen copy as reference, G6,temperature1.0/retry1.15,LR5e−6,beta.04,40+40updates,assistant-onlyloss,ctx4096,192tokens/turn. Dropout was disabled during GRPO scoring.\n\nThe first5updates accepted5/9groups (55.56%), with finite gradients and9.86GiBpeakreservedVRAM. Full acceptance was80/342 (23.39%); Stage1 40/148 (27.03%) andStage2 40/194 (20.62%).260zero-variance and3all-negative rejected groups overlap once;257of262skips were all scalar/status-correct. Accepted groups:68mixed-outcome,7all-correct with process variation,5all-wrong with positive evidence bridges;9contained format errors. Each stage accepted15conflict,15incident,5consistent,5abstain groups, without fallback. The retained journal has2052sampled episodes and480optimized episodes. The acceptance ratio is2.807× the old8.33% pilot, but G and temperatures also changed: this does not isolate a shorter-SFT entropy effect.\n\nValidation20/40/60/80 was73.75/80.00/76.25/78.75%, with predicted abstention26.25/25.00/23.75/23.75%. No >40%guard fired. Best40 was restored and holdout-evaluated; final80 was separately saved. Stage2 did not beatStage1. Frozen-reference parameter L1 change was0; Stage2 policy L1 change was809.235549; all80 logged gradient norms were finite. Peak observed GRPO/validation reservedVRAM wasabout13.51GiB.\n\nExecution used two capped3h L40S allocations: preparation+SFT+Stage1, then exact40-update policy handoff into a fresh Stage2optimizer as specified by the original recipe. A provisional Stage2update before the first cancellation was archived and replayed, not counted twice. No SFT/baseline phase was repeated for that handoff.')
